From aa6324594a3e3d90c8dc411110f25719bcd57ce6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <reaper@local>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 23:24:32 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] www: shared device-name resolver + unified byte formatters +
 theme-token canon (D1/D3/D4)

D1 - one shared device-name resolver (reaper_util.js). The get_clientlist +
nickName||name resolver was reimplemented three ways across the Reaper-native
pages (Traffic: lc-MAC + IP, seeded from custom_clientlist; Conn: IP-only; QoS:
lc-MAC). reaper_util.js#ReaperNames is the single implementation - one index keyed
by BOTH lowercase MAC and IP, seeded from the persistent custom_clientlist master
store (so OFFLINE devices keep their user-assigned name - the v2.2.5 Traffic
behaviour, now shared by Conn and QoS too) then overwritten by the freshest live
nickName. Reaper_Traffic/Conn/QoS now delegate to it (each call site keeps its own
fallback: '' for Traffic/Conn, the MAC for QoS). The server-side custom_clientlist
string is still injected per-page via the ASP nvram_char_to_ascii tag (a .js file
is not ASP-processed) and passed to refresh(). Stock index.asp / Advanced_DHCP use
the trivial inline nickName||name field and are intentionally left (refactoring
stock dashboard/DHCP for a one-liner is poor risk/reward).

D3 - one decimal-SI byte formatter + the bit-rate formatters (reaper_util.js#ReaperFmt).
Traffic and Conn each carried their own fmtBytes/fmtRate copies that had drifted in
decimals; both now delegate to ReaperFmt (decimal SI: 1 MB = 1,000,000 B, per owner
decision). Both bit-rate label styles ("Mb/s" and "Mbps") are provided so no page's
rate label text changes. QoS bandwidth CAPS keep their own 1024 bit formatter by
design (a bitrate cap is a different quantity). Only visible change: Conn byte totals
now show SI decimals (e.g. 5 GB -> 5.00 GB).

D4 (partial) - canonicalize accidental same-name/different-value theme-token drift
across the 15 Reaper pages: --body quoted 'Segoe UI' (8 pages, renders identically),
--amber #d9a441 -> #E0A93B (6 pages), --danger #E10600 -> #FF3B30 (1 page, FirstBoot).
The naming-vocabulary consolidation (--panel2/--red -> --panel-2/--crimson) and the
--line cream-vs-red divergence require rewriting each page's CSS usages and stay
deferred to the native-page migration.

L1 - closed as not-a-bug (no code change): the leading '<' in the set_name/set_reserve
RMW output is the correct, required stock list format for custom_clientlist and
dhcp_staticlist (every parser strtok/strseps on '<'); removing it would corrupt both.

Bump EXTENDNO to Reaper_v2.2.7.
